<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Cuthbert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 634–687</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Cuthbert of Lindisfarne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Cuthbert was a Northumbrian monk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>, the most venerated saint of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids his community carried his relics to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester-le-street">Chester-le-Street</a> and in 995 to Durham, where his shrine became the centre of the cathedral. The lands and privileges of the community of Saint Cuthbert passed to the bishops of Durham and underpinned their later palatine power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-cuthbert.jpg" alt="Saint Cuthbert" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bede · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuthbert_discovers_piece_of_timber_-_Life_of_St._Cuthbert_(late_12th_C),_f.45v_-_BL_Yates_Thompson_MS_26.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
